K14 PASS sigma8: 3.346 at delta_c=0, 0.760 at 1.0, 0.760 at 1.686, 0.760 at 4.0; delta_c(0.832) = 0.677   monotone decreasing, root in (0.02, 6)
      the threshold reading sweeps from the full pointwise law at delta_c -> 0 (sigma8 = 3.35: worse than A40's 2.267, because RESP ~ delta^-1/2 diverges as delta -> 0 and the 8/h Mpc mode starts from zero) to the inert branch at delta_c -> infinity. It has one free parameter and no derivation
K15 PASS delta_c(0.832) = 0.677;  delta = 1 at 8/h Mpc;  delta_collapse = 1.686;  ratio = 0.40;  sigma8 at delta_c = 1.686: 0.7602   0.1 < delta_c(0.832) < 1
      *** the bound-system reading cannot deliver sigma8 = 0.832. ***  To reach Planck the modification must switch on in the QUASI-LINEAR regime, at delta ~ 0.5-0.8, a factor 2-3 below the collapse threshold. At the bound-system scale (delta_c = 1.686) the sigma8 mode never triggers it and the prediction is the inert 0.760, 6.3% low. So 'the law acts only in bound systems' is consistent with the data but not preferred by them
K16 PASS sigma8(delta_c=1.686) = 0.7602 vs inert 0.7602; diff = -0.0000   |diff| < 5e-3
      the 8/h Mpc sphere reaches delta = 0.76 at z = 0, so a threshold at 1.686 is never crossed and the growth is exactly the inert one. The reading is self-consistent and empty: it converts the fork into a nonlinear-scale statement with no linear signature

