      EH98 no-wiggle (zero-baryon), n_s = 0.965, amplitude fixed by the same Planck anchor A40 uses. k_NL = 0.22 h/Mpc is the standard Planck value, which calibrates the shape. The shape is only needed at the few-% level: the conclusion below is a factor 479
      linear field: max_R g_R = 2.361e-13 m/s^2 = 0.0021 a0 at R = 21.54 Mpc
K18 PASS max g_R/a0 = 0.0021 at R = 21.5 Mpc; shortfall = 479x   max g_R < a0
      *** a0 is not a cosmic acceleration. ***  The rms smoothed linear field peaks at R ~ 20 Mpc at 0.21% of a0; at every other scale it is smaller (0.18% even at 100 Mpc, because sigma(R) R is almost flat past the turnover). There is no R at which 'the mean smoothed field equals a0' in the linear universe. Whatever sets the MOND transition in cosmology, it is not a linear smoothing of the density field
      forcing the filter on the linear field: G_eff/G in [11.5, 291.1] (deep MOND at every R)
K19 PASS G_eff/G in [11.5, 291.1] for R in [1 kpc, 10 Gpc] -> sigma8 ~ 2.27   G_eff > 3 at every linear scale
      smoothing a linear mode does not reduce its field: a 15 kpc filter applied to a 100 Mpc mode returns the mode's own field. So the filtered reading at linear scales IS branch (b); the filter changes nothing. This is the check that kills the naive 'environmental' rescue of linear growth
      nonlinear field: R(a0) from an isothermal halo with g = 2 sigma^2/r = a0:
