        v_c = 300.0 km/s -> R(a0) =  51531.8 pc =  51.53 kpc ;  k(a0) =     28.8 h/Mpc
K20 PASS R(a0) = 12883 pc for v_c = 150 km/s (range 1431-51532 pc); k(a0) = 115 h/Mpc   k(a0) in [10, 1e4] h/Mpc
      the a0 transition happens where the field really is a0, and that is inside galaxies: R(a0) = 2 sigma^2/a0 = 1.4-52 kpc for v_c = 50-300 km/s. Hence k(a0) = 29-1037 h/Mpc, two to four decades above every linear scale (k <~ 0.2 h/Mpc). The modification is a sub-grid, object-by-object effect; the PM engine CFG960 applies it exactly this way (y = G M_b(<r)/r^2 at the local r)
K21 FAIL sigma8(0) = 0.7602 (adopted 2.2668); f(0) = 0.45224; 1/R(a0) = 115 h/Mpc vs k_lin <= 0.2 h/Mpc   adopted == G_eff=1 to 1e-6
      the filtered and the inert branch coincide: both are GR on the theory's background, because the only places the filtered field reaches a0 are k ~ 1e2 h/Mpc, where the linear growth equation is not the right description anyway. sigma8 = 0.760, and f(z) is the LCDM shape on the theory's background

